  "title": "Matatu to Town",
  "category": "transport",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "level": 5,
  "order": 504,
  "theme": "transport",
  "culturalNote": "Matatus are Nairobi's privately run minibuses — loud music, graffiti art, and a makanga (conductor) hanging out of the door shouting the route. Fares aren't fixed: they rise at rush hour and when it rains, so it's normal to ask \"Nauli ni ngapi?\" before you get in. You get off by shouting where you want to stop. Imperatives are the plain verb: Panda! (get in), Shuka! (get off), Simama! (stop).",
  "startingPoint": "You're at the stage in Kilimani. A makanga is shouting for passengers to town.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "future-ta",
    "imperatives",
    "numbers-money"
  ],
